  "transcript": "# Regulatory sandboxes in financial services\n\n## What the ScaleUp Institute does and why sandboxes matter\n> Sandboxes let companies test regulated products with realistic data, helping them refine offers and access markets.\n\nChair: Please introduce yourself and explain how the ScaleUp Institute engages with regulatory sandboxes.\nIrene Graham: I'm CEO of the ScaleUp Institute. We work across the UK to help companies scale—addressing access to markets, talent, finance and infrastructure—and we advise governments, local authorities and firms.\nIrene Graham: Sandboxes matter because they let fintechs and banks test products using realistic or live data within a regulatory setting so they can refine services and understand regulatory requirements before full market launch.\nLord Sharkey: When are sandboxes most useful, and when might other regulatory engagement be better?\nIrene Graham: They're useful at both start-up and later stages. For new services or partnerships with large banks you want to test in a regulated, data-rich setting. But innovation pathways and other routes can work alongside sandboxes; it isn't either/or.\nLord Sharkey: Has anyone measured whether sandboxes actually deliver outcomes? The FCA's last big review was in 2017.\nIrene Graham: We track outcomes and can share evidence. Many firms that tested in sandboxes later raised funds or formed partnerships. Not every test succeeds, but successful validation in a sandbox often helped firms attract investment.\n\n\n## Who uses sandboxes and what they gain\n> Around 200 firms have entered the FCA sandbox; firms use it to validate products, win partners, and signal credibility to investors.\n\nLord Hollick: Start-ups we heard from said the sandbox duplicates authorisation work and costs them early on. Do you see that?\nIrene Graham: Some firms experience duplication and cost, which is valid feedback, but many benefit: testing yields validation that supports fundraising. The experience varies by firm and product.\nLord Turnbull: How many fintechs are there and how many have been through the sandbox?\nIrene Graham: About 2,500 fintech firms nationwide. Roughly 200 businesses have entered the FCA sandbox so far; we can provide updated figures and case studies.\nLord Turnbull: Are entrants mainly small start-ups or established companies testing new lines?\nIrene Graham: Both. The sandbox serves early-stage firms and established players. It's roughly balanced, maybe skewed toward smaller firms, and firms can use it more than once.\nLord Lilley: Could the apparent benefit you describe be because your members are already later-stage and better placed to use the sandbox?\nIrene Graham: Some of our members are later-stage, but benefits are seen across stages. Usage and success depend on firm specifics, so the picture is nuanced.\nLord Turnbull: Can you name sandbox success stories?\nIrene Graham: Examples include Zilch and Salary Finance. We can supply more names and evidence of funding or partnerships that followed sandbox participation.\n\n\n## Costs, alternatives and improvements\n> Firms worry about cost and duplication; improvements include the FCA's scale-up unit, continuous access, clearer links to authorisation, and faster market routes.\n\nBaroness Bowles of Berkhamsted: Are accelerators before or after sandboxes? At what stage should a firm go into a sandbox?\nIrene Graham: There’s no fixed order. Accelerators, bank programmes and the sandbox are part of a richer set of options. Firms choose the route that fits their product and readiness.\nBaroness Donaghy: You praised the FCA's new scale-up unit and say sandboxes are now open-access rather than cohort-only. How does that help firms?\nIrene Graham: Open access removes a timing barrier and the scale-up unit provides account management—direct regulatory contact for fast-growing firms. That should reduce friction and help navigation.\nBaroness Donaghy: What remains slow or costly and how fast should it be?\nIrene Graham: The slow parts are the links between testing, formal authorisation and getting into procurement or markets. Faster pathways are needed where a product is proven—especially to reach buyers like large banks or public bodies.\nLord Hill of Oareford: Can sandboxes be improved further?\nIrene Graham: Yes. Keep them, broaden digital and AI testing, keep improving access, and work internationally to speed market access. The scale-up unit is a helpful recent development.\nLord Griffiths of Fforestfach: The US scales faster without sandboxes. Why is that?\nIrene Graham: The US advantage is depth of growth capital and large-scale institutional funding. Singapore and others also learned from the UK's sandbox model; market size and capital matter a lot.\nIrene Graham: Also, corporate venturing and acquisitions are more common in the US, which speeds scaling via partnerships and buy‑outs—something the UK could encourage further.\n\n\n## Selection, transparency and next steps\n> Selection is competitive but open; transparency needs balance between learning and protecting commercial data; the Institute will provide follow-up evidence.\n\nLord Davies of Brixton: Does the FCA pick winners by choosing cohort members for challenge panels, which could exclude good ideas?\nIrene Graham: The challenge route is like any open competition: applicants submit and a selection is made on merit. Firms can reapply or use other routes; having multiple access channels helps avoid unfair exclusion.\nLord Smith of Kelvin: Are sandboxes transparent enough so the wider market benefits from lessons learned?\nIrene Graham: There's room to improve. Regulators should publish aggregated lessons while protecting commercially sensitive or personal data. Balancing public insight and competitive confidentiality is key.\nChair: You offered to send more evidence on outcomes and successful firms. Anything else you’d add?\nIrene Graham: We'll send detailed case studies, funding and partnership outcomes. Also note: some unicorns now use the sandbox even if they didn't scale solely because of it; evidence will show the range of impacts.\nChair: Thank you—this has been helpful. We'll expect your follow-up evidence on numbers, success stories and how sandboxes link to authorisation and market access.\n",
